const commonExtensions = {
  en: [
    [["Know where QC evidence comes from", "Inspection aggregators commonly receive photos, videos, weights, dimensions and defect notes from third-party agents or public sources. That makes the record useful, but not self-authenticating. Check that the marketplace, item link, option and date belong together. A platform can standardize and de-duplicate records without being the photographer, seller or certifier. If the source chain is unclear, lower your confidence instead of filling the gap with an assumption."], ["Compare records, not one perfect frame", "A single unit can be unusually good or unusually bad. When several records exist, compare repeated characteristics: toe shape, panel alignment, print placement, zipper path, measurement method and the same high-stress seams. Repetition is more informative than one dramatic close-up. Still, historical consistency cannot guarantee the next batch, so keep conclusions limited to what the records actually show."], ["Treat lighting and perspective as variables", "Warehouse lighting, white balance, compression and a wide-angle phone lens can alter color and proportions. Compare neutral references in the frame, such as a ruler, white label or background, and check the same feature from another angle. Ask for a targeted photo when color or shape controls the decision. Do not call a difference a defect until it survives a second view."], ["Use category-specific priorities", "For shoes, prioritize size label, insole or outsole length, paired symmetry, sole attachment and heel alignment. For clothing, prioritize flat measurements, fabric surface, print position, seams and hardware. For watches or small accessories, inspect moving parts, clasp, edges, finish and included components. A fixed generic checklist is the baseline; the product category decides where most risk sits."], ["Record observations in neutral language", "Write what is visible before writing the verdict: ‘left print sits lower by about five millimetres’ is more useful than ‘looks bad.’ Add the image angle, measurement and requested action. Neutral notes make a second reviewer faster and reduce emotional decisions. They also help later if the live listing changes, because the record preserves what you evaluated rather than pretending the photos proved more than they did."]],
    [["Separate same-item groups from similar results", "A search system may group records that are likely to be the same item and show visually similar recommendations beside them. These are different confidence levels. Same-item grouping is still a probability, while a similar result is mainly a discovery lead. Before transferring a conclusion, compare the marketplace, seller or source, product option, construction, title and destination URL. Visual resemblance alone is not a reliable product identifier."], ["Handle dead or changed listings", "A historical QC page can survive after the original listing is removed, redirected or edited. That record may still teach you about construction, measurement or common defects, but it cannot prove that the item is currently purchasable. Preserve the old URL and date, then look for an active source whose title, images, options and details can be verified independently. Never relabel an alternative as the original simply because it looks close."], ["Use image search as a candidate generator", "Image search is valuable when a link is dead or a title is vague, but it ranks visual similarity rather than commercial identity. Cropping, background removal, colorways and reused seller photos can create false matches. Start from the least edited image available, inspect multiple candidates and promote a candidate only after the destination page confirms the same visible specifications. A high visual match is an invitation to check, not a conclusion."], ["Check freshness before relying on metadata", "Price, stock, options, seller timing, domestic delivery and return information can change. Treat every displayed value as time-stamped, even when the interface does not show a prominent date. Open the live listing before purchase, repeat the check before warehouse action and save the chosen option. Freshness matters most when a decision depends on availability, a return window or a route restriction."], ["Avoid false precision in search notes", "Do not describe a result as exact unless you can state why: recognized source URL, matching item ID, same option and consistent destination. Use labels such as exact source, likely same item, visually similar or unverified. This simple vocabulary prevents a research page from turning ranking confidence into a factual claim. It also gives readers a useful next action—open, compare, request evidence or discard the candidate."]],
    [["Identify which weight you are reading", "A record may show product-only weight, weight with retail packaging, warehouse intake weight or final parcel weight. Those figures answer different questions. Product weight helps an early estimate; the final consolidated measurement is the relevant starting point for shipping. Write the measurement stage beside every number so that two legitimate values are not mistaken for an error. Third-party weight and dimension fields should be checked for plausibility, not treated as guaranteed measurements."], ["Measure the final outer dimensions", "Volumetric calculations use the external parcel dimensions, including bulges and protective corners, not the folded dimensions of the item. A small increase on three sides can materially change the dimensional figure. After consolidation, inspect the length, width and height supplied for the finished carton. If the package is irregular, confirm how the route measures the largest points and whether its system rounds each side before calculating."], ["Model more than one packing choice", "Make a conservative scenario with retail boxes and protection, and a reduced-volume scenario with removable packaging excluded. Compare the saving with the lost protection, presentation and possible return value. Shoes, rigid gifts and delicate hardware may justify space that soft clothing does not. The goal is not the smallest parcel at any cost; it is the lowest reasonable billable size that still protects the items you chose."], ["Compare the complete route rule", "For each eligible line, record the billable basis, divisor, rounding unit, minimum charge, maximum side, combined-dimension limit, restricted item rules and surcharges. Then calculate with the same final parcel in every row. A route with a lower per-kilogram rate can lose after a minimum, a large-parcel surcharge or harsher rounding. Use current checkout figures because rules and availability can change by destination."], ["Audit before payment", "Pause when the consolidated result differs sharply from your estimate. Recheck item count, removed packaging, measured mass, dimensions, selected services and route. If the service supports it, request a reweigh or packaging photo for an implausible value. Keep customs, tax and import restrictions as a separate check: weight influences cost, but it does not determine whether the contents are legal or how the destination will assess them."]]
  ]
};

